{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "rp-02-proportion-and-missing-proportionals",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Proportion and Missing Proportionals",
    "description": "Use equality of ratios and cross-products to find a missing term or proportional mean.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the ratio model from the quantities named, explain why each transformation preserves or changes it, show intermediate steps and checks, and use fresh practice with targeted remedial retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 45,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 13 “Ratio and Proportion,” printed pages 426–474. Approved scope: Define a:b::c:d as equality a/b=c/d where denominators are non-zero; name means and extremes and derive the product rule ad=bc. Find fourth proportional d, third proportional c, and positive mean proportional √(ab) where the domain permits; include fractional and decimal terms and distinguish a mean proportional from an arithmetic mean. Check every result in the original proportion and reject invalid zero-denominator cases. Use opening concepts and solved examples 3 and 14–15 plus objective questions asking for missing terms or proportional means."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Proportion and Missing Proportionals: Use equality of ratios and cross-products to find a missing term or proportional mean. This lesson builds the ratio model, works through its reasoning, then checks understanding with fresh practice and targeted recovery.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A proportion states that two ratios have equal values: a:b::c:d means a/b=c/d. The denominators b and d must be non-zero for this fraction interpretation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Cross multiplication gives ad=bc. In a:b::c:d, a and d are extremes while b and c are means; the product of extremes equals the product of means.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For the fourth proportional to a,b,c, set a:b=b? No: the required relation is a:b::c:x, so ax=bc and x=bc/a when a is non-zero.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: the fourth proportional to 4, 9, and 12 satisfies 4:9::12:x. Thus 4x=108 and x=27; substitution gives 4/9=12/27.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A third proportional x to a and b satisfies a:b::b:x, so ax=b². A mean proportional m between positive a and b satisfies a:m::m:b, hence m²=ab and m=√ab.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Choose cross-products rather than memorizing a placement rule. Write the four terms in order, mark the two diagonal products, and solve the resulting one-step equation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For a decimal or fractional term, retain exact fractions if possible. For example, 0.4:x::2:5 gives 0.4×5=2x, so x=1; check the original ratios equal 0.4.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Do not cross-multiply terms from a different order. If the statement is a:b::c:d, then the equation is ad=bc; swapping b and c changes the proportion unless their values happen to match.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The mean proportional is a geometric mean, not an arithmetic mean. Between 4 and 9 it is √36=6; the arithmetic mean 6.5 does not satisfy 4:6.5::6.5:9.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check sign and domain. A positive mean proportional is defined for non-negative product, and the textbook’s ordinary quantity problems use the positive root; algebraic equations may require stating both roots if negatives are allowed.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A proportion can be checked both as a cross-product equality and as equal fractions. The cross-product is usually faster; the fraction check helps catch a misplaced term.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When solving for a missing term, estimate first. In 4:9::12:x, the second term is larger than the first, so the fourth term should exceed 12; the result 27 is plausible.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Use proportionality only when the relationship is truly multiplicative. A fixed additive difference does not preserve a ratio; translate words like “more than” before deciding whether a proportion applies.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "In mixed problems, solve the missing proportional first, then use it in the later ratio or total. Keep the intermediate term exact rather than rounding and carrying an avoidable error.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "You should now be able to identify a proportion, solve a missing term, and distinguish fourth, third, and mean proportionals. Verify the final term in the exact relationship that defined it.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Decide which quantities form the ratio and keep their order visible. Keep the four terms in their given order. Use the given relationships before substituting numbers.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
